{{Top}}
{{Quote|As you may know, I am ''See-Threepio'', a protocol droid fluent in over six million forms of communication. But even this spectacular achievement does not represent the total number of languages present in our galaxy. There are many others—some used only by very small populations, or others lost to the winds of time.|C-3PO|<ref name="SW9">[[Star Wars (2020) 9|''Star Wars'' (2020) 9]]</ref>}}
[[File:Language-Thrawn1.png|thumb|300px|[[Mitth'raw'nuruodo]] "Thrawn" speaks in Galactic Basic Standard before switching for Sy Bisti to say "preemptive strikes," a phrase he did not yet know in Basic.]]
A '''language''' was a method of [[Sentience|sentient]] communication, either oral, [[Writing|written]] or [[Sign language|sign]], consisting of the use of sounds, words and/or physical gestures in a structured and conventional way.

Prevalent languages in [[the galaxy]] included [[Galactic Basic Standard]] and [[Bocce]]. Languages like [[Huttese]], [[Aqualish (language)|Aqualish]], and [[Mando'a]] dominated in systems controlled by those societies.<ref name="Visual Encyclopedia">''[[Star Wars: The Visual Encyclopedia]]''</ref>

Some languages used more than one script. For instance, Galactic Basic used the [[Aurebesh]], [[Outer Rim Basic]], and [[High Galactic]] forms of written communication depending on the situation. Additionally, most societies used a base ten number system, however, there are exceptions. [[Jawa]]s used a base nine system, and the [[Hutt]]s counted on their fingers, of which they only have eight, and their numbers jump from seven to ten.<ref name="Visual Encyclopedia" />

In order to overcome language barriers between beings from different [[species]] or ethnic groups,<ref name="Databank-Amda Wabo">{{Databank|amda-wabo|Amda Wabo}}</ref> [[protocol droid]]s were equipped with formidable language skills.<ref name="Databank-Protocol Droid">{{Databank|protocol-droid|Protocol Droid}}</ref> When communicating among several species, a [[trade language]] such as [[Sy Bisti]]<ref name="Thrawn novel">[[Thrawn (novel)|''Thrawn'']]</ref> or [[Jawa Trade Talk]] could be used.<ref name="AbEverything">''[[Star Wars: Absolutely Everything You Need to Know]]''</ref>

To some, [[Galactic Credit Standard|credits]] were considered a universal language.<ref name="Rebel Rising">''[[Rebel Rising]]''</ref>

[[List of phrases and slang|Phrases and slang]]<ref name="High 7 2021">[[The High Republic (2021) 7|''The High Republic'' (2021) 7]]</ref> were a part of language.

==Behind the scenes==
===Aurebesh inserts===
Some on-screen [[Aurebesh]] texts contain excerpts from other, real world languages:

====German====
In the ''[[Star Wars: The Clone Wars]]'' episode "[[Liberty on Ryloth]]," an all-Aurebesh [[Galactic Republic|Republic]] monitor includes the text "UND UBERHAUPT,"<ref name="Liberty">{{TCW|Liberty on Ryloth}}</ref> [[Wikipedia:German language|German]] for "and anyway."

====Italian====
In the ''Star Wars: The Clone Wars'' episode "[[The Academy]]," when [[Soniee]] and her fellow [[Imperial Academy of Mandalore|students]] break into the [[Government warehouse]], her [[datapad]] says "ENTRARE" in Aurebesh,<ref name="Academy">{{TCW|The Academy}}</ref> which is [[Wikipedia:Italian language|Italian]] for "enter."<ref name="SW-The Academy Trivia Gallery">{{SW|url=series/clone-wars/the-academy-trivia-gallery|text="The Academy" Trivia Gallery|quote=Once Soniee slices through the lock on the warehouse, her computer screen says "Entrare," which is Italian for "enter."}} {{C|Slide 6}}</ref>

====Spanish====
In the ''[[Star Wars: Visions]]'' episode "[[T0-B1 (short film)|T0-B1]]," the big monitor in [[Professor]] [[Mitaka]]'s laboratory is filled with Aurebesh text. Most of it is written in [[Wikipedia:Spanish language|Spanish]]; for example, there is "PERMANENCIA DESARROLLO DATOS," Spanish for "continuation development data"; and "ATMOSFERA MINERALES,"<ref name="T0-B1" /> meaning "[[atmosphere]] [[mineral]]s." When [[T0-B1]] continues the professor's work, he fills the monitors with notes. Most of them are a transcription of<ref name="T0-B1">{{Visions|T0-B1 (short film)}}</ref> excerpts from the lyrics of the [[2018]] song "[[Wikipedia:Malamente|Malamente]]" by the Spanish singer [[Wikipedia:Rosalía (singer)|Rosalía]],<ref name="Malamente">{{WebCite|url=https://genius.com/Rosalia-malamente-cap1-augurio-lyrics|text=MALAMENTE (Cap.1: Augurio)|archivedate=20220417055543}}</ref> but with some spelling errors.<ref name="T0-B1" /> For example, one excerpt from the original song reads "Ese cristalito roto yo sentí cómo crujía / Antes de caerse al suelo ya sabía que se rompía,"<ref name="Malamente" /> but the show's version reads "Ese ''cris talito'' roto yo ''sen cmo'' crujía antes ''d ecaerse'' al suelo ''y asaba'' que se rompía".<ref name="T0-B1" />

====Norwegian====
One of the [[Unidentified lab tech (Plazir-15)|lab tech's]] readouts in ''[[Star Wars: The Mandalorian]]'' episode "[[Chapter 22: Guns for Hire]]" contains two paragraphs written in [[Wikipedia:Norwegian language|Norwegian]]. The first one is reads:<ref name="Chapter 22">{{TheMandalorian|Chapter 22: Guns for Hire}} {{C|Note that the ''top'' line in the ''first'' [[Wikipedia:Norwegian language|Norwegian]] paragraph is only seen from behind, and is therefore mirror-inverted; while the ''entire'' second Norwegian paragraph is only seen clearly from behind; when it is seen from the front, it is too blurry to decipher}}</ref>

<center>
{|class="wikitable" width="auto" style="text-align: left";
|-
|<center>'''Transcription'''</center>
|<center>'''English translation'''<ref name="Translation">Translated by user: Nightlily, a native speaker of [[Wikipedia:Norwegian language|Norwegian]]</ref></center>
|-
|<poem>DEN NORSKE SKOGKATTEN ER ET
PERFEKT HUSDYR FOR EN MIDDELS
AKTIV FAMILIE. HVIS DU GIR DEN ET
INNEN

DORS<sup>'''*'''</sup> <small>''[sic]''</small> KLATRETRE OG MYE
KJAERLIGHET KAN DERE HA EN GOD
VENN I MANGE AAR.</poem>
|<poem>The [[Wikipedia:Norwegian Forest cat|Norwegian Forest cat]] is a
perfect pet animal for a moderately
active family. If you give it an
in

door <small>''[sic]''</small> climbing tree and lots of
love, you can have a good
friend for many years.</poem>
|}</center>

<sup>'''*'''</sup><small>Note that "innendors" (spelled "innendørs" in Norwegian), the Norwegian word for "indoor", has incorrectly been split into two words.</small>

The second paragraph consists of<ref name="Chapter 22" /> lyrics from the Norwegian national anthem, "[[Wikipedia:Ja vi elsker dette landet|Ja vi elsker dette landet]]" ("Yes, we love this country"):<ref name="FM">{{WebCite|url=https://www.classicfm.com/discover-music/periods-genres/national-anthems/norway-history-english-lyrics|text=What is the national anthem of Norway, and what are the lyrics in English?|date=2022-02-04|work=[[Wikipedia:Classic FM (UK)|Classic FM]]|quote=Norway's official national anthem is 'Ja, vi elsker dette landet' ('Yes, we love this country') (...) It has been in use since the mid-1860s, but wasn't officially recognised as the nation's national anthem until just a few years ago in 2019. (...)<br /><br />Yes, we love this country<br />as it rises forth,<br />rugged, weathered, over the water,<br />with the thousands of homes, —<br />love, love it and think<br />of our father and mother|archivedate=20220705220559 }}</ref>

<center>
{|class="wikitable" width="auto" style="text-align: left";
|-
|<center>'''Transcription, with typos'''</center>
|<center>'''Correct lyrics<ref>{{WebCite|url=https://genius.com/Bjrnstjerne-bjrnson-ja-vi-elsker-dette-landet-annotated|text=Ja, vi elsker dette landet|quote=Ja, vi elsker dette landet,<br />som det stiger frem,<br />furet, værbitt over vannet,<br />med de tusen hjem, —<br />elsker, elsker det og tenker<br />på vår far og mor|archivedate=20221023111140}}</ref></center>
|<center>'''English translation'''<ref name="FM" /></center>
|-
|<poem>{{uc:Ja vi elsker dette
landet
som det stiger frem
furet vaer bit}} <small>''[sic]''</small> {{uc:over
vannet med det}} <small>''[sic]''</small>
{{uc:tusen hjem

elsker, elsker det a}} <small>''[sic]''</small>
{{uc:tenker paa var far og
mor}}</poem>
|<poem>Ja, vi elsker dette
landet,
som det stiger frem,
furet, '''værbitt''' over
vannet, med '''de'''
tusen hjem,

elsker, elsker det '''og'''
tenker på vår far og
mor</poem>
|<poem>Yes we love this
country,
as it rises forth,
rugged, weathered over
the water, with the
thousand homes,

love it, love it and
think of our father and
mother</poem>
|}
</center>

Finally, "ALLIANSEN", which is Norwegian for "the alliance,"<ref name="Translation" /> is written vertically in a different readout.<ref name="Chapter 22" />

